-- Passage rg11-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4a94ef71-c422-5acd-9e2a-57dc871d959e', 'rg11-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Canal or the Railroad: Brennick, 1846$q$, $q$In the winter of 1846, the merchants of Brennick, a river town of about four thousand people, faced a decision that would shape its future. A private company had offered to build either a canal linking Brennick to the port city of Halsey or a railroad along the same route. The town would have to buy shares to fund the project, and it could afford only one.

The canal's leading champion was a grain dealer named Ezra Pollock. In a pamphlet distributed that January, he argued that canals had proven themselves over two decades, while railroads were still an experiment. He pointed out that a single canal boat could carry as much grain as forty wagons, and that the company's own estimates put the cost of shipping by canal at half the cost by rail. He also claimed that a railroad would frighten livestock and ruin the farms along its path, though he offered no examples of this happening anywhere.

The railroad's supporters answered in a pamphlet signed by a schoolteacher, Adaeze Morrow, and eleven merchants. They did not dispute Pollock's cost figures. Instead, they shifted the question. The canal, they noted, would freeze for nearly four months each winter, when Brennick's mills would be cut off from Halsey entirely. A railroad could run all year. They added that a train could make the trip in six hours instead of three days, which mattered for goods like butter and cheese that spoil.

Morrow's pamphlet also argued that because two neighboring towns had recently voted for railroads, Brennick would be left behind if it chose otherwise. Pollock replied that following the neighbors was no substitute for counting costs.

In March, the town voted for the railroad by a narrow margin. Within fifteen years, the canals in the region had lost most of their traffic to rail lines. Historians still debate whether Brennick chose wisely for the right reasons or simply guessed well.$q$, 'draft')
