   $q$Which statement best evaluates the author's use of the butterfly count?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It is weak because volunteers, not scientists, did the counting.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misnames-flaw"},{"text":"It is weak because butterflies do not feed on native flowers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It is convincing because a 150 percent increase is a very large change.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misled-by-percentage"},{"text":"It is weak because two small counts on one six-mile stretch cannot prove a countywide reversal.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It is weak because two small counts on one six-mile stretch cannot prove a countywide reversal.$q$, $q$A rise from 12 to 30 butterflies at a single site over two years is far too little to "prove" anything about the whole county, and the percentage makes a change of 18 butterflies sound dramatic. Who did the counting is a lesser issue than how little was counted.$q$, 'draft', 'af3ff9d2-309b-5cb0-bcc9-27bddf2a66eb')
